diagnosis_text:
I tested the end-to-end path between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) and could not reproduce any packet loss. From pc1 to pc2 and back, sustained 60‑packet tests at short intervals (-i 0.05) showed 0% loss with stable sub‑millisecond RTTs. The topology‑wide reachability check also reported 0% loss in both directions. On the inter‑AS routers, BGP adjacency is healthy and stable: router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) each show their peer up, 1 prefix received, and correct routes installed (router1 has 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 has 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). Interface and queueing statistics on all links (eth0 and eth1 on both routers and hosts) show zero errors and zero drops, and there are no firewall policies that would explain selective loss.

Mechanistically, if roughly one third of packets were being dropped on the inter‑AS path, we would expect to see interface drops, tc qdisc drops, BGP flaps, or asymmetric/RPF issues—none are present. Localization therefore points to no active fault along router1↔router2 or host links.

Root cause: not reproducible at this time; likely a transient convergence or measurement artifact earlier.

Corrective fix: no device restart is warranted. Continue monitoring; if symptoms recur, capture pc1↔pc2 pings alongside router1/router2 interface counters to pinpoint any transient behavior. 
=== END ===